      "case": "filesystem/glob/match-byte-vs-rune",
      "dialect": "lsp",
      "discriminates": "The two arms that look alike and are not. anyChar consumes one BYTE (glob.go:300 input = input[1:]), so ? does not match \"\\u00e9\" and ?? does -- a port that consumes a rune passes every rendering row and fails both of these. charRange decodes a RUNE and advances by its size (glob.go:320, :324), so [\\u00e0-\\u00e9] matches the two-byte \"\\u00e9\" whole. And the negate flag is read by nothing: [!a-z] matches \"b\" TRUE and \"A\" false, exactly like [a-z], so a port that implements the negation documented at glob.go:34-36 reverses both answers. [\\ufffd-a] parses but matches nothing, including U+FFFD itself, and a literal \\xff matches itself byte-wise (glob.go:259 HasPrefix), which a port holding literals as String cannot do.",
