      "case": "filesystem/glob/read-range-rune-decode",
      "dialect": "lsp",
      "discriminates": "Catches the value-then-size gate. readRangeRune does not error on the size alone: the whole switch is inside `if r == utf8.RuneError` (glob.go:140), and only a RuneError decode reaches the size arms (glob.go:142-147). A properly encoded U+FFFD decodes to RuneError with size 3 and is accepted, so a port that rejects the rune U+FFFD rejects the legal pattern [\\ufffd-a]; an invalid byte decodes to RuneError with size 1 and is rejected, so a port that accepts any RuneError accepts [\\xff-\\xff]; and an empty input decodes with size 0 and is rejected with the OTHER error, errBadRange rather than errInvalidUTF8. A decode that is NOT RuneError never errors whatever its size: action 01 records \"a\" as rune 97, size 1, no error against action 03's \"\\xff\" as rune 65533, size 1, invalid UTF-8 -- same size, opposite outcome, decided by the value. A port that switched on the size alone would return errInvalidUTF8 for every ASCII bound and fail to parse [a-z] at all. The size is load-bearing and otherwise unobservable: the '[' arm advances with pattern = pattern[sz:] twice (glob.go:113, :122), so a port returning 1 for a multi-byte bound would land mid-rune and read the wrong delimiter.",
